#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""claude-move: move one running Claude to another subscription, same conversation.

  claude-move <session id> <account> [--model M] [--continue | --signed-out | --asking | --wake] [--force]
                       [--json]

What claude-account does, without its menu, and the auto-swap watcher's hands. Copies the transcript into the other account,
stops the Claude, starts it there with --resume: a background Claude in a window through its wrapper (the record's
`switch`), one without a window with `claude --bg --resume`, a plain Claude by Ctrl+C twice, /exit and a relaunch in
its own tab. The same account with --model only changes the model. The model it ran is kept unless --model says
otherwise; either way a conversation over 150 000 tokens keeps the 1M context.

  --continue    sends "continue" once it runs again (a reply a limit cut off). To the account the Claude is already on
                it is the swapper's wake after that account's limit reset: the same stop and `--bg --resume`, restarted
                where it is, with no transcript copy and no account change. Refused ("no longer stopped at its limit")
                unless the Claude is still stopped at that limit.
  --signed-out  --continue for a Claude whose account was signed out: the same move, the same refusals.
  --asking      moves a Claude that is waiting on a question, because its account is spent: only while the question is
                as it was asked (nothing typed into it); once it runs again it is told it was moved and asks the same
                question again. A permission prompt is never moved.
  --wake        tells the Claude, once it runs again, that and why it was moved, in a message that starts with
                a bracketed tag; what it was in the middle of picks the wording (working, question, limit, signed-out,
                warned, background or none).

A blank session id is refused at once (exit 2, "no session id"). Refuses (exit 3, nothing touched): not running; the
account it is already on (unless --model, or --continue); busy or waiting on you (unless --force, or --asking for
waiting); text in its prompt that was not sent (unless --force); already being moved by another call on the same
session (a per-session lock); a move to an account whose first run was never finished, or that was never told to trust
the folder the Claude would start in (--force bypasses neither). If it will not start on the other account it is
started again where it was (exit 1).

A Claude already stopped is never simply left gone: a failure after the stop is a best-effort restart where it was, and
the report says plainly whether that restart worked. For a Claude in a window the window's wrapper makes the restart
(the switch is turned around to name the old account); for one without a window the mover does, but only when it
knows nobody else will and that the Claude runs nowhere; a list it cannot read is never "nothing runs".

Before anything stops the Claude a marker (state/claude-hub/claude-move/<session id>: epoch seconds and the target
account) says a move is ending it. What belongs to the conversation goes to the other account with it, before the stop
and again after it: the conversation's own folder (with subagents/ and tool-results/), its task list and its file
history (a hard link where the filesystem allows, never a symlink, a lock file or a half-written file; the newest file
wins). If it comes up under a new session id those are copied to the new id. A failure in any of it is a note in the
result's reason and never stops the move.

A row counts as the moved Claude only when it is that Claude for sure, never merely because it is new. A Claude not
seen like that in time is a failed move: nothing is reported or written under a session id that was not seen. A failed
move names the Claude where it runs now whenever the mover knows which one that is.

Every write of a window's record holds one lock (the file .records.lock in the records folder, tried every 0.05 s for
at most CLAUDE_HUB_RECORD_LOCK_WAIT seconds, 5 by default), so a clear and the wrapper's read cannot interleave and
start two copies of one conversation.

A background Claude the daemon has just resumed is a fork under a new session id whose transcript file does not exist
until its first new message; its daemon roster entry names the conversation it was forked from, so with no transcript
of its own it moves with that one, copied under the Claude's own id.

Exit: 0 moved, 1 failed (it runs where it was), 2 usage, 3 refused.
Test switches: CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_TIMEOUT, CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_WAIT_TIMEOUT, CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_STOP_TIMEOUT,
CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_WINDOWED_WAIT, CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_ATTACH_OF, CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_SLICE_WAIT, CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_LOOK_WAIT,
CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_WENT_LOOK_WAIT, CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_TAIL_RESERVE, CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_START_MIN, CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_ASK_TIMEOUT,
CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_ASKED_WAIT, CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_JOB_WAIT, CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_BUDGET, CLAUDE_HUB_MOVE_CLOSE_WAIT,
CLAUDE_HUB_RECORD_LOCK_WAIT, plus claude-desks's.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import codecs
